  push(Eq([frac('d', 'du'), mr(' '), sub('q', 'u'), mr('(B) = '), frac('1', sub('A', 'u')), mr(' ∫ ('), sub('1', 'B'), mr(' − '), sub('q', 'u'), mr('(B)) '), sup('e', 'ρ'), mr(' d(p′ − p).')]));
  push(P0(`The integrand (1_{B} − *q*_{u}(*B*)) *e*^{ρ} takes values in [1 − *q*_{u}(*B*), (1 − *q*_{u}(*B*))*e*^{o}] on *B* and in [−*q*_{u}(*B*)*e*^{o}, −*q*_{u}(*B*)] off *B*, so its oscillation is at most *e*^{o}; and the integral of a function of oscillation at most *c* against a signed measure of total mass zero is at most *c* times half the total variation of that measure, that is, *c*‖*p*′ − *p*‖_{TV}. Hence |d*q*_{u}(*B*)/d*u*| ≤ *e*^{o}‖*p*′ − *p*‖_{TV}, and integrating from 0 to 1 gives |(*T*_{ρ}*p*′)(*B*) − (*T*_{ρ}*p*)(*B*)| ≤ *e*^{o}‖*p*′ − *p*‖_{TV} for every *B*, which is the second inequality. For the last assertion, fix a site *y* and a configuration ω of the other sites, and let *p*_{ω} be the conditional law of the variable at *y* under *W*; the conditional law under *W e*^{δ} is *T*_{ρ}*p*_{ω} with ρ the restriction of δ to the variable at *y* at ω, of oscillation at most *o*_{y}(δ). If ω′ differs from ω only at *y*′, with restriction ρ′, then *T*_{ρ′}*p*_{ω′} = *T*_{ρ′−ρ}(*T*_{ρ}*p*_{ω′}), so by the triangle inequality and the two inequalities just proved, ‖*T*_{ρ}*p*_{ω} − *T*_{ρ′}*p*_{ω′}‖_{TV} ≤ *e*^{o}‖*p*_{ω} − *p*_{ω′}‖_{TV} + 1 − *e*^{−osc(ρ′−ρ)} ≤ *e*^{o}*C*(*y*, *y*′) + 1 − *e*^{−Δ}, with *o* = *o*_{y}(δ) and Δ = Δ_{yy′}(δ). ∎`, { before: 0 }));
  push(Thm('Hypothesis I', 'The blocked bound', [
    `There are numbers κ > 0 and α < 1 and a rung j₀ such that at every rung j ≥ j₀, on every lattice of the construction, Σ_{y′} C^{λ}_{j}(y, y′) e^{κ|y−y′|} ≤ α for every coarse point y.`,
  ]));
  push(Thm('Hypothesis II', 'The fibre clustering', [
    `There are a number ρ′ < 1, a constant K′ and a rung j₀ such that at every rung j ≥ j₀, on every lattice of the construction, for every coarse configuration v and all bounded observables f and g, with d the distance between S(f) and S(g):`,
    `(a) |ν_{j}(fg | v) − ν_{j}(f | v) ν_{j}(g | v)| ≤ K′ N(f) ‖f‖ ‖g‖ ρ′^{d};`,
    `(b) the fibre mean F(v) = ν_{j}(f | v), as a function of the coarse configuration, satisfies δ_{y}(F) ≤ K′ N(f) ‖f‖ ρ′^{r} for every coarse point y, where r is the distance from y to S(f).`,
  ]));
  push(P0(`Hypothesis I is the second link of §8.1 in its finite form: a statement about the conditional law of one coarse variable at one fixed physical scale, with the exponential weight that fixes the range, and with constants independent of the cutoff and of the volume. Hypothesis II is the ultraviolet half of the chain at the scales below *a*_{c}: with the coarse variables held fixed, the field inside the blocks clusters at the block scale, and its means depend on distant coarse variables only through an exponentially small tail. It is demanded for every coarse configuration, the adversarial ones included, because a single-site criterion conditions on the worst case; this is where it exceeds the small-field expansions, which control the fluctuation field in typical backgrounds and the large fields by their probability, and it is named here as the demand it is. Neither hypothesis refers to a limit. Their consequence is the gap.`, { before: 60 }));
  push(Thm('Theorem 8.5', 'The reduction of the gap to the blocked bound', [
    `Let a construction be as in Theorem 7.4 with L = 2, blocked at the coarse scale a_{c} with a block map as above, and suppose Hypotheses I and II. Put ρ = max(e^{−κ}, ρ′). Then for every ρ₁ with ρ < ρ₁ < 1 there is a constant K, depending on ρ, ρ₁, α, K′, N(f), N(g), ‖f‖ and ‖g‖ and on nothing else, such that at every rung j ≥ j₀, on every lattice of the construction, for all bounded observables f and g, |S_{j}(fg) − S_{j}(f) S_{j}(g)| ≤ K ρ₁^{d}, with d the distance between S(f) and S(g); and every limit point of the construction's Schwinger functions, on the sector generated by the observables that are bounded uniformly in j, has a unique vacuum and a Hamiltonian with no spectrum in (0, m), where`,
  ]));
  push(Eq([mr('m = '), frac('1', sub('a', 'c')), mr(' min ( κ, log '), frac('1', 'ρ′'), mr(' ).')], '8.8'));
  push(Proof([
    `Fix *j* ≥ *j*₀, a lattice, and bounded observables *f* and *g*; write *F*(*v*) = ν_{j}(*f* | *v*) and *G*(*v*) = ν_{j}(*g* | *v*) for their fibre means, bounded by ‖*f*‖ and ‖*g*‖. The covariance splits along the block map: μ_{j}(*fg*) − μ_{j}(*f*)μ_{j}(*g*) = [λ_{j}(*FG*) − λ_{j}(*F*)λ_{j}(*G*)] + λ_{j}(ν_{j}(*fg* | ·) − *FG*). The second term is the λ_{j}-mean of the covariance of *f* and *g* under the fibre measure, at most *K*′*N*(*f*)‖*f*‖‖*g*‖ρ′^{d} by Hypothesis II(a).`,
    `For the first term put *G*′ = *G* + 2‖*g*‖, with values between ‖*g*‖ and 3‖*g*‖, and λ′ = *G*′λ_{j}/λ_{j}(*G*′). The blocked measure is a finite system of coarse variables with compact values, and Dobrushin's comparison theorem applies to it:[[fn:R. L. Dobrushin, "Prescribing a system of random variables by conditional distributions", *Theory Probab. Appl.* 15 (1970), 458–486, where the uniqueness theorem and the comparison theorem are proved for arbitrary single-site spaces; the comparison theorem is used here in the form of Theorem 8.20 of H.-O. Georgii, *Gibbs Measures and Phase Transitions*, second edition, de Gruyter, Berlin, 2011. Its finite form, for finite single-site spaces, is among the theorems checked by machine in §8.2.]] if λ′ has conditional laws that differ from those of λ_{j} at *y* by at most *b*_{y} in total variation, for every conditioning, then for every bounded function *F* of the coarse configuration, |λ′(*F*) − λ_{j}(*F*)| ≤ Σ_{i,y} δ_{i}(*F*) *D*(*i*, *y*) *b*_{y}, where *D* = Σ_{n≥0} (*C*^{λ}_{j})^{n}. The conditional law of λ′ at *y* is that of λ_{j} tilted by log *G*′ as a function of *v*_{y}, so by the first inequality of Proposition 8.4 the two differ by at most *b*_{y} = 1 − *e*^{−osc} ≤ osc ≤ δ_{y}(*G*′)/inf *G*′ ≤ δ_{y}(*G*)/‖*g*‖ ≤ *K*′*N*(*g*) ρ′^{r′}, where osc is the oscillation of log *G*′ in *v*_{y} and *r*′ the distance from *y* to *S*(*g*), by Hypothesis II(b). Under Hypothesis I the weighted matrix *C̃*(*y*, *y*′) = *C*^{λ}_{j}(*y*, *y*′) *e*^{κ|y−y′|} has row sums at most α, and since |*y* − *y*″| ≤ |*y* − *y*′| + |*y*′ − *y*″|, induction on *n* gives (*C*^{λ}_{j})^{n}(*y*, *y*′) *e*^{κ|y−y′|} ≤ *C̃*^{n}(*y*, *y*′) ≤ α^{n}; so *D*(*y*, *y*′) ≤ *e*^{−κ|y−y′|}/(1 − α). With δ_{i}(*F*) ≤ *K*′*N*(*f*)‖*f*‖ρ′^{r} by Hypothesis II(b), *r* the distance from *i* to *S*(*f*), the comparison theorem gives`,
  ], 'Proof', true));
  push(Eq([mr('| λ′(F) − '), sub('λ', 'j'), mr('(F) | ≤ '), sub('∑', 'i,y'), mr(' '), sub('δ', 'i'), mr('(F) D(i, y) '), sub('b', 'y'), mr(' ≤ '), frac(mr('K′² N(f) N(g) ‖f‖'), mr('1 − α')), mr(' '), sub('∑', 'i,y'), mr(' '), sup('ρ', 'e(i,y)'), mr(',')]));
  push(P0(`where *e*(*i*, *y*) = *r*_{i} + |*i* − *y*| + *r*′_{y}, with *r*_{i} the distance from *i* to *S*(*f*) and *r*′_{y} that from *y* to *S*(*g*), and ρ = max(*e*^{−κ}, ρ′) bounds the base of each of the three factors. By the triangle inequality *e*(*i*, *y*) ≥ *d*, and the number of pairs (*i*, *y*) with *e*(*i*, *y*) ≤ *n* is at most *N*(*f*)*N*(*g*)(2*n* + 1)^{8}, since *i* then lies within distance *n* of *S*(*f*) and *y* within distance *n* of *S*(*g*). Hence the last sum is at most *N*(*f*)*N*(*g*) Σ_{n≥d} (2*n* + 1)^{8} ρ^{n}, which is ρ^{d} times a polynomial in *d* and is therefore at most *K*₁ρ₁^{d} for every ρ₁ ∈ (ρ, 1), with *K*₁ depending on ρ, ρ₁, *N*(*f*) and *N*(*g*). Since λ_{j}(*FG*) − λ_{j}(*F*)λ_{j}(*G*) = λ_{j}(*G*′)(λ′(*F*) − λ_{j}(*F*)) and λ_{j}(*G*′) ≤ 3‖*g*‖, the first term is at most 3*K*′²*N*(*f*)*N*(*g*)‖*f*‖‖*g*‖*K*₁ρ₁^{d}/(1 − α). Adding the second term gives the covariance inequality, with *K* = 3*K*′²*N*(*f*)*N*(*g*)‖*f*‖‖*g*‖*K*₁/(1 − α) + *K*′*N*(*f*)‖*f*‖‖*g*‖.`, { before: 0 }));
  push(P(`For the gap, let *f* be an observable, bounded uniformly in *j*, whose links lie in the time interval [0, *w*]. The links of θ*f̄* lie in [−*w*, 0] and those of τ_{t}*f* in [*t*, *t* + *w*], and the block boundaries in time are the multiples of *a*_{c}, so the distance between *S*(θ*f̄*) and *S*(τ_{t}*f*) is at least ⌊*t*/*a*_{c}⌋ ≥ *t*/*a*_{c} − 1. The covariance inequality gives *S*_{j}(θ*f̄* · τ_{t}*f*) − |*S*_{j}(*f*)|² ≤ (*K*/ρ₁) *e*^{−m₁t} with *m*₁ = log(1/ρ₁)/*a*_{c}, the constant independent of *j* and of the lattice because *N*(*f*) and ‖*f*‖ are. This is the hypothesis of Theorem 7.4 with *r*_{j} = 0 at every *t* ∈ *D*, so Theorem 7.4 leaves no spectrum in (0, *m*₁) for every ρ₁ ∈ (ρ, 1), hence none in (0, *m*) with *m* = log(1/ρ)/*a*_{c}, which is (8.8). ∎`, { before: 0 }));
  push(P(`Hypothesis I is read on the effective action of the coarse variables. Write the blocked measure as λ_{j}(*dv*) = *e*^{−A}*dv* with respect to the product over the blocks of a fixed reference measure on *V*, so that *A* = *A*_{j}(*v*) is the effective action at the coarse scale, and let Δ_{yy′}(*A*) be its mixed second difference in the sense of Proposition 8.4: the supremum, over coarse configurations differing only at *y*′, of the oscillation in *v*_{y} of the difference of the two actions. The conditional law of *v*_{y} under one of the two configurations is the tilt of its conditional law under the other by that difference, so the first inequality of Proposition 8.4 gives *C*^{λ}_{j}(*y*, *y*′) ≤ 1 − *e*^{−Δ} with Δ = Δ_{yy′}(*A*). Hypothesis I is therefore a bound on the mixed second differences of the effective action between blocks, weighted by their distance: the influence functional of §8.1, and the number that a certificate of the second link must deliver at the coarse scale.`, { before: 120 }));
  push(P(`One might wish to state the hypothesis on the fine measure directly, taking the blocks as the sites of a Gibbs measure and the whole configuration of a block as its variable, so that Dobrushin's condition for that measure would give every local observable's decay at once, with no fibre clause. That form is not uniform in the cutoff. Its interdependence coefficient between adjacent blocks is the total variation between two laws of all the links of a block under outside data that differ on a whole neighbouring face; as the spacing tends to zero that face carries ever more links, the two laws become distinguishable almost surely by an average over the face, and the coefficient tends to one at every coupling, so that the row sums tend to at least one. Whole-block total variation is the one norm that does not survive large blocks, and the hypothesis of the reduction is therefore stated on the coarse variables, whose type is fixed, with the field inside the blocks entering through its clustering alone.`, { before: 120 }));
  push(P(`Theorem 8.5 is the second link in the form the route attacks it. Hypothesis I is a bound on the blocked measure at the coarse scale, the object named in §8.1 as the influence functional of the blocked action. Hypothesis II is what the ultraviolet half must deliver below that scale; for the gauge group SU(2) the literature states nothing in this form, so it stands beside Hypothesis I as the second half of what the crossover must supply, and it is named as such. Proposition 8.4 is the fourth link in its exact form. The blocked measures λ_{j} have variables of one type at every rung, so it is meaningful to say that their conditional laws at the coarse scale are tilts of those of one rung *j*₁ by functions δ_{j} whose oscillations *o*_{j} and weighted mixed differences Σ_{y′}(1 − *e*^{−Δ})*e*^{κ|y−y′|} tend to zero; when they are, Hypothesis I at the rung *j*₁ with the constant α₁ implies it at every later rung with any α ∈ (α₁, 1), by (8.7). That the measures stand in this relation is the ultraviolet interface of §8.1, and it is the place at which Bałaban's theorem enters the chain; the two statements the corpus leaves unwritten there, and the discharge of the theorem's own hypothesis on the sequence of couplings, are theorems on this work's path (§8.3).`));
  push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured: Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and from β = 2.3 the fundamental character coefficient falls below the finite-group threshold 0.131 of §8.2 at the seventh step, from β = 5 at the eighteenth, and from β = 10 at the thirty-seventh.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent; the instrument, its records at the four starting couplings and the run of record are listed in the Records. The first step from β = 2.2 is read from the same record: the coefficient rises from 0.464 to 0.486, an effective coupling of 2.34, where the one-loop running of ℤ⁴ takes 2.2 to 1.94 under one doubling of the scale.]] The record also says what the model is not. Its first step from β = 2.2 moves the coupling up where the running of ℤ⁴ moves it down, by an amount of the step's own size, so the model certifies the entry into the disk from its fifth rung and the threshold at its seventh, and not the crossover's first steps, which a flow on ℤ⁴ must read on an object of ℤ⁴. The distance Hypotheses I and II must cross is measured in the blocking steps of ℤ⁴ itself; no step count of the hierarchical model is a reading of it, and no reading of the hierarchical model is a statement about ℤ⁴. One more thing is known about the inequality that would carry the model's control to ℤ⁴. A comparison that bounded the mixed second differences of the effective action of ℤ⁴ by those of the hierarchical model, and held for every compact gauge group, would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap wherever its hierarchical flow reaches the disk; the U(1) theory has a massless phase at weak coupling.[[fn:A. H. Guth, "Existence proof of a nonconfining phase in four-dimensional U(1) lattice gauge theory", *Phys. Rev. D* 21 (1980), 2291–2307; J. Fröhlich and T. Spencer, "Massless phases and symmetry restoration in abelian gauge theories and spin systems", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 83 (1982), 411–454.]] The inequality the step needs therefore depends on the group, and that dependence is the first property a proof of Hypothesis I must use.`));
  push(P(`Two things are known about the strong end of that distance. With the first-order transmission between links, the total variation moved by one neighbour's change, no block criterion of Dobrushin and Shlosman's kind gains on the single link at any block side, because a link deep inside a block branches to its eighteen neighbours exactly as a link of the full lattice does, and the criterion's series has the single link's ratio. A block gains only through the transmission across an integrated face, which carries the product of the two pinned means of the face's other edges, an average over the interior: its one-face constant is an explicit function of the moments of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere, the covariance of the transmitted variable being a multiple of the identity plus one rank-one term whose size is a closed form in two Bessel ratios, certified in a run of record. What is not in hand is the theorem that composes that transmission along paths inside a block: every composition that is proved, Dobrushin's comparison theorem among them, takes a supremum over configurations at each step and so composes the first-order sensitivities only, which return the single link's disk; a convergent expansion of the block measure would compose the averages, and its radius has no proof beyond that disk.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the block criterion's accounting, the one-face law and the closed form of its covariance are entered on the ledger of the work that follows this paper, with their readings on the exact cube.]] The paper carries no number for that widening until both are theorems.`));
  push(P(`One averaged composition is rigorous, and it is an instance of Theorem 8.5 in which the fibre clause holds exactly. Call a set of links of ℤ⁴ a *perfect cover* when every plaquette contains exactly one of its links. One exists with period two in every direction: reading the coordinates of a site *x* modulo 2, take the link from *x* in direction 1 when *x*₂ = *x*₃ = *x*₄, in direction 2 when *x*₄ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₃, in direction 3 when *x*₂ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₄, and in direction 4 when *x*₃ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₂. The four conditions partition the sixteen residues, so the set has one link at every site and density one quarter, and a direct check of the ninety-six plaquettes of the torus of side two shows that each contains exactly one of its links; since the condition is local and the set periodic, the same holds on ℤ⁴ and on every torus of even side.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the cover was found by an exhaustive search on the torus of side two and checked again, on a road sharing no line with the search, on every plaquette of the tori of side two, four and six, where a single bent site leaves ten plaquettes of the smallest torus with zero or two links of the set; Records R7.]] Two consequences are used below: no plaquette contains two links of the cover, and a link outside the cover lies in six plaquettes whose six cover links are distinct, since two links share at most one plaquette.`));
  push(Thm('Lemma 8.6', 'The constants of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere', [
    `For w in ℝ⁴ let ν_{w} be the law on the unit sphere S³ of ℝ⁴, identified with SU(2), whose density with respect to the uniform measure is proportional to e^{⟨u, w⟩}. Write r = |w|, Z(r) = 2I₁(r)/r for its normalising constant, φ = log Z and m = φ′ = I₂/I₁, with I_{ν} the modified Bessel functions. The mean of ν_{w} is m(r)ŵ; its covariance has the eigenvalue φ″(r) on ŵ and m(r)/r on the orthogonal complement; and its third cumulant along a unit vector u with ⟨u, ŵ⟩ = c is φ‴(r)c³ + 3(D(r)/r)c(1 − c²), where D = φ″ − m/r. For every r ≥ 0:`,
    `(i) m(r)/r ≤ 1/4 and φ″(r) ≤ 1/4;`,
    `(ii) −m(r)²/3 ≤ D(r) ≤ 0;`,
    `(iii) the third cumulant of ν_{w} along every unit vector is at most m(r)/2 in absolute value.`,
  ]));
  push(Proof([
    `The cumulants of ν_{w} are the derivatives at *w* of *x* ↦ φ(|*x*|) − φ(|*w*|), the logarithm of the normalised Laplace transform of ν_{w}. Its gradient at *w* is *m*ŵ and its Hessian has the stated eigenvalues; along the line *x* + *tu* the radius ρ(*t*) = |*x* + *tu*| has ρ′ = *c*, ρ″ = (1 − *c*²)/*r* and ρ‴ = −3*c*(1 − *c*²)/*r*² at *t* = 0, so the third derivative φ‴ρ′³ + 3φ″ρ′ρ″ + φ′ρ‴ is the stated form. The recurrences *I*₁′ = *I*₂ + *I*₁/*r* and *I*₂′ = *I*₁ − 2*I*₂/*r* give φ′ = *m* and the Riccati identity`,
  ], 'Proof', true));
