  push(P0(`Put *z* = *r*²/4 and *A* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 2)!), *B* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 1)!), *C* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 3)!), so that *A* = (2/*r*)²*I*₂, *B* = (2/*r*)*I*₁ and *C* = (2/*r*)³*I*₃, all three positive, with *m*/*r* = *A*/(2*B*) and *m*² = *zA*²/*B*². Comparing coefficients gives *B* − 2*A* = *zC* ≥ 0, which is the first half of (i). By (8.9), φ″ = 1 − 3*A*/(2*B*) − *zA*²/*B*², and the substitution *B* = 2*A* + *zC* gives`));
  push(Eq([frac('1', '4'), mr(' − φ″ = '), frac(mr('z (A² − ¾ BC)'), mr('B²')), mr(',      D = φ″ − '), frac('m', 'r'), mr(' = − '), frac(mr('z (A² − BC)'), mr('B²')), mr('.')], '8.10'));
  push(P0(`Now *A*² − *BC* = (2/*r*)⁴(*I*₂² − *I*₁*I*₃). The product formula *I*_{μ}*I*_{ν} = Σ_{k} (*r*/2)^{μ+ν+2k} (μ + ν + 2*k*)!/(*k*!(μ + *k*)!(ν + *k*)!(μ + ν + *k*)!)[[fn:G. N. Watson, *A Treatise on the Theory of Bessel Functions*, second edition, Cambridge University Press, 1944, §5.41.]] makes the *k*-th coefficient of *I*₂² − *I*₁*I*₃ equal to (4 + 2*k*)!/(*k*!(4 + *k*)!(2 + *k*)!(3 + *k*)!), which is positive, and that of 3*I*₁*I*₃ − 2*I*₂² equal to *k*/(2 + *k*) times the *k*-th coefficient of *I*₁*I*₃, which is not negative. Hence 0 ≤ *A*² − *BC* ≤ *A*²/3. The left inequality is Turán's inequality for the modified Bessel functions.[[fn:K. V. Thiruvenkatachar and T. S. Nanjundiah, "Inequalities concerning Bessel functions and orthogonal polynomials", *Proc. Indian Acad. Sci. A* 33 (1951), 373–384.]] By (8.10) it gives *D* ≤ 0, and, since then ¾*BC* ≤ ¾*A*² ≤ *A*², it gives φ″ ≤ 1/4, the second half of (i). The right inequality gives |*D*| ≤ *zA*²/(3*B*²) = *m*²/3, which is (ii). For (iii), differentiate (8.9): φ‴ = 3|*D*|/*r* − 2*m*φ″, where 0 ≤ 3|*D*|/*r* ≤ *m*²/*r* ≤ *m*/4 by (ii) and (i), and 0 ≤ 2*m*φ″ ≤ *m*/2 because φ″ is a variance at most 1/4. With *a* = 3|*D*|/*r* and *b* = 2*m*φ″, the third cumulant at *c* in [0, 1] is *ac*(2*c*² − 1) − *bc*³, and it is odd in *c*. When 2*c*² ≥ 1 it lies between −*bc*³ ≥ −(*m*/2)*c*³ and *ac*(2*c*² − 1) ≤ (*m*/4)*c*(2*c*² − 1); when 2*c*² < 1 it equals −(*ac*(1 − 2*c*²) + *bc*³), whose size is at most (*m*/4)*c*(1 − 2*c*²) + (*m*/2)*c*³ = (*m*/4)*c*. In every case its size is at most (*m*/4)*c*(1 + *c*²) ≤ *m*/2. ∎`));
  push(Thm('Theorem 8.7', 'A lattice mass gap at strong coupling; Edwin Boston III', [
    `Consider the SU(2) lattice gauge theory on ℤ⁴ with Wilson's action, each plaquette weighted by exp(½ β tr U_{p}), at 0 < β ≤ 0.241. Its infinite-volume Gibbs measure is unique. For every m₀ < log(1/α(β))/2, with α(β) ≤ 0.9963 the number of (8.12), there is a constant K, depending on β and m₀ alone, such that for all bounded observables f and g depending on finitely many links, |⟨fg⟩ − ⟨f⟩⟨g⟩| ≤ K N(f) N(g) ‖f‖ ‖g‖ e^{−m₀ d}, with N(f) the number of links on which f depends and d the least distance, in the maximum norm, between the midpoints of a link of f and a link of g; the same bound holds on every torus of even side. The Hamiltonian H = −log T of the transfer matrix T annihilates a unique vacuum and has no spectrum in the interval (0, m₀).`,
  ]));
  push(Proof([
    `Let *L*₁ be the perfect cover and *L*₀ its complement, and read each element of SU(2) as a unit vector of ℝ⁴, so that ½ tr *U*_{p} = ⟨*U*_{a}, *S*_{p}(*a*)⟩ for the cover link *a* of a plaquette *p*, the staple *S*_{p}(*a*) being the product of the other three links of *p*, or of their inverses, read as a unit vector. Each plaquette contains exactly one link of *L*₁, so the Wilson weight is the product over *a* in *L*₁ of exp⟨*U*_{a}, η(*a*)⟩, with η(*a*) = β Σ_{p∋a} *S*_{p}(*a*) and |η(*a*)| ≤ 6β. Given the links of *L*₀, the links of *L*₁ are therefore independent, *U*_{a} distributed by ν_{w} at *w* = η(*a*), and the marginal law of the links of *L*₀ has density proportional to ∏_{a∈L₁} *Z*(|η(*a*)|) with respect to the product of the Haar measures: a Gibbs measure with a potential of finite range. For *x* in *L*₀ and a plaquette *p* ∋ *x*, let *a*_{p} be the cover link of *p*, θ_{p}(*U*_{x}) = β*S*_{p}(*a*_{p}), which is β times an isometry of ℝ⁴ applied to *U*_{x}, and *w*_{p} = η(*a*_{p}) − θ_{p}(*U*_{x}), the contribution of the five other plaquettes of *a*_{p}, so that |*w*_{p}| ≤ 5β. The conditional law of *U*_{x} given the other links of *L*₀ is proportional to ∏_{p∋x} *Z*(|*w*_{p} + θ_{p}(*U*_{x})|), the six factors coming from six distinct cover links.`,
    `By Taylor's theorem along the segment from *w*_{p} to *w*_{p} + θ_{p}, which stays in the ball of radius 6β, and since *m* is increasing, log *Z*(|*w*_{p} + θ_{p}|) = φ(|*w*_{p}|) + ⟨*m*(|*w*_{p}|)ŵ_{p}, θ_{p}⟩ + ½⟨θ_{p}, Σ_{p}θ_{p}⟩ + *R*_{p}, with Σ_{p} the covariance of ν_{w} at *w* = *w*_{p} and |*R*_{p}| ≤ (β³/6)*T*₆, *T*₆ = *m*(6β)/2, by Lemma 8.6(iii). The linear terms add to ⟨*U*_{x}, *v*⟩ for a vector *v* to which *p* contributes a vector of length at most β*m*(5β); the quadratic term of *p* equals a constant plus ½β²*D*(|*w*_{p}|)⟨θ_{p}/β, ŵ_{p}⟩², whose oscillation in *U*_{x} is at most ½β²*D*₅, with *D*₅ = *m*(5β)²/3, by Lemma 8.6(ii). The conditional law of *U*_{x} is therefore the tilt of ν_{v} by a function *h* = Σ_{p} *h*_{p} of oscillation at most`,
  ], 'Proof', true));
  push(Eq([mr('ω = 3'), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(' + 2'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr('.')], '8.11'));
  push(P0(`Two facts bound the influence of one link on that law. First, if *p* is a probability measure and *g* a bounded function of oscillation *o*, the tilt *T*_{g}*p* differs from *p* in total variation by at most tanh(*o*/4) ≤ *o*/4: with *g* normalised to take values in [0, *o*], for a set *B* of *p*-measure *s* on which *e*^{g} averages *a* and off which it averages *a*′, (*T*_{g}*p*)(*B*) − *p*(*B*) = *s*(1 − *s*)(*a* − *a*′)/(*sa* + (1 − *s*)*a*′), which is largest at *a* = *e*^{o}, *a*′ = 1 and *s* = 1/(1 + *e*^{o/2}), where it equals tanh(*o*/4). Second, by the sharp one-link lemma of §8.1, the mean absolute deviation of ⟨*U*, *e*⟩ under any law ν_{v} is at most 4/(3π) for every unit vector *e*, and under a tilt of ν_{v} by a function of oscillation at most ω it is at most (3*e*^{ω} − 1)/2 times as large: if the tilt has density *g* with values in [*c*, *ce*^{ω}] with respect to ν_{v}, where *c* ≤ 1, and *Y* = ⟨*U*, *e*⟩ − ν_{v}(⟨*U*, *e*⟩) with *M* = ν_{v}(|*Y*|), the deviation is at most ν_{v}(*g*|*Y*|) + |ν_{v}(*gY*)| ≤ *ce*^{ω}*M* + *c*(*e*^{ω} − 1)*M*/2, because ν_{v}(*Y*⁺) = *M*/2. Differentiating along the segment from *v* to *v*′, two tilts of ν_{v} and ν_{v′} by one function of oscillation at most ω differ in total variation by at most ρ(ω)(2/(3π))|*v* − *v*′|, with ρ(ω) = (3*e*^{ω} − 1)/2.`));
  push(P(`Now let a link *y* of *L*₀ change, the others fixed. If *y* is one of the two other links of a plaquette *p* of *x*, as twelve links are, then *v* moves by at most 2β*m*(5β), because a factor of the isometry moves by at most 2, and *h*_{p} moves by a function of oscillation at most β²*D*₅ + (2/3)β³*T*₆. If *y* lies in one of the five other plaquettes of a cover link *a*_{p}, as ninety links do, fifteen for each of the six, then *w*_{p} moves by at most 2β, so *v* moves by at most β·¼·2β = β²/2, the Jacobian of the mean being the covariance, whose norm is at most ¼ by Lemma 8.6(i); the quadratic part of *h*_{p} moves by a function of oscillation at most min(β²*D*₅, 2β³*T*₆), the second bound because the covariance moves in norm by at most 2β times the bound on the third cumulant, the norm of a symmetric trilinear form being its supremum on the diagonal; and the remainder moves by at most (2/3)β³*T*₆. By the two facts the interdependence coefficients are at most`));
  push(Eq([sub('C', 'dir'), mr(' = ρ(ω) '), frac('4β', '3π'), mr(' m(5β) + '), frac('1', '4'), mr('( '), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(' + '), frac('2', '3'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(' ),')]));
  push(Eq([sub('C', 'med'), mr(' = ρ(ω) '), frac(sup('β', '2'), '3π'), mr(' + '), frac('1', '4'), mr('( min('), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(', 2'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(') + '), frac('2', '3'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(' ),      α(β) = 12 '), sub('C', 'dir'), mr(' + 90 '), sub('C', 'med'), mr('.')], '8.12'));
  push(P0(`A link *y* that occupies several of these positions is counted once for each, which bounds its coefficient because total variation is subadditive along a chain of single changes, and no other link of *L*₀ affects the law of *U*_{x}; the column sums obey the same count. Every term of α is increasing in β, and Table 3 gives its value: α(0.241) = 0.996297 < 1. By Dobrushin's uniqueness theorem the Gibbs measure of the links of *L*₀ is unique,[[fn:R. L. Dobrushin, "Prescribing a system of random variables by conditional distributions", *Theory Probab. Appl.* 15 (1970), 458–486; Sources [17].]] and, since the links of *L*₁ are conditionally independent given them with a law that is fixed, so is the Gibbs measure of the theory. The coefficients vanish between links at distance more than two, so the weighted row sums of Hypothesis I are at most α*e*^{2κ}, which is less than one for κ < log(1/α)/2; Hypothesis II holds with zero fibre covariance for observables with disjoint supports, whose links in *L*₁ are distinct and conditionally independent, and with fibre means that depend on the links of *L*₀ within distance one of their support. The argument of the proof of Theorem 8.5, applied on one lattice with the links of *L*₀ as the coarse variables, gives the clustering bound with any *m*₀ < log(1/α)/2, uniformly over the tori of even side and in the infinite volume. The Wilson action is reflection positive and its transfer matrix is strictly positive,[[fn:K. Osterwalder and E. Seiler, Sources [5]; M. Lüscher, Sources [7].]] the bound applies to θ*f̄* and τ_{t}*f* at every integer time *t*, and Theorem 7.1, applied to the reconstruction at integer times, gives the vacuum and the gap. ∎`));
